spec bulletproofs_verify

description:
  Bulletproofs range-proof verification. Given a Pedersen commitment `V` (or a
  list of commitments for an aggregated proof), a bit length `k` (<= 64), and a
  proof `pi`, `verify_range` returns `true` if and only if `pi` is a valid
  proof that every committed value `v_j` satisfies `0 <= v_j < 2^k`, under the
  Fiat-Shamir transcript binding all public inputs.

  Covers `tpt-crypto-zk::bulletproofs` (`verify_range`,
  `range_proof_from_bytes`), the inner-product argument (`src/ipa.rs`), the
  Pedersen generators, and the SHAKE256 Fiat-Shamir `Transcript`. Verification
  is over public data only.

inputs:
  commitments : &[Point]   // Pedersen commitments V_j = [v_j] G + [gamma_j] H
  bits        : usize      // range bit length k, 1 <= k <= 64
  proof       : &[u8]      // serialized proof, attacker-controlled

outputs:
  result : Result<bool, ZkError>   // Ok(true) iff valid; Ok(false) / Err on failure

assumes:
  - `G`, `H`, and the generator vectors are the fixed nothing-up-my-sleeve set
  - the verifier transcript is seeded identically to the prover's
  - `commitments.len()` is a power of two for an aggregated proof

ensures:
  // completeness: an honest proof of in-range values always verifies
  ( for all j. 0 <= v_j < 2^bits )  &&  proof == honest_prove_range(v, gamma, bits)
      =>  result == Ok(true)
  // soundness: acceptance implies the committed values are in range (up to the
  // discrete-log / knowledge-soundness assumption of the argument)
  result == Ok(true)
      =>  exists v, gamma. ( for all j. commitments[j] == [v_j] G + [gamma_j] H
                             && 0 <= v_j < 2^bits )
  // any tampered proof, wrong commitment, or wrong bit length fails, not panics
  tampered(proof) || wrong(commitments) || wrong(bits)  =>  result != Ok(true)
  malformed(proof)                                      =>  result is Ok(false) or Err
  // determinism: verification is a pure function of its public inputs
  verify_range(c, k, pi) == verify_range(c, k, pi)

non_leaks:
  - verification has no secret inputs (values and blindings are the prover's);
    no constant-time obligation applies to `verify_range`
  - proof deserialization rejects non-canonical scalars / off-curve points
    before any group operation; a parse failure is a returned error, never UB

verification:
  // pending: bulletproofs module not yet building/tested. Targets:
  // dalek-compatible reference range-proof vectors in tests/kat/, plus
  // proptest: honest prove_range always verifies; out-of-range value =>
  // prove fails; single-bit proof mutation => verify fails.
